    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "> *AI authored to showcase ironpad capabilities.*\n\n# Langton's Ant\n\n**Langton's Ant** is a two-dimensional Turing machine with remarkably simple rules that produce surprisingly complex behavior. Invented by Chris Langton in 1986, it operates on a grid of black and white cells.\n\n## Rules\n\nThe ant follows just two rules based on the color of its current cell:\n\n1. **On a white cell**: Turn **right** 90°, flip the cell to **black**, move forward.\n2. **On a black cell**: Turn **left** 90°, flip the cell to **white**, move forward.\n\n## Emergent Complexity\n\nStarting from a blank grid, the ant's path appears **chaotic** for the first ~10,000 steps, creating a seemingly random blob. Then, suddenly, it begins building a diagonal **\"highway\"**, an infinitely repeating pattern of 104 steps that carries the ant away in a straight line.\n\nThis abrupt transition from chaos to order is one of the most striking examples of emergent behavior in simple systems. No one has proven *why* the highway always appears.\n\n## This Notebook\n\nWe simulate 12,000 steps on a 200×200 grid. Black cells appear dark, and the ant's current position is highlighted in red.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Simulation & Render",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\nconst SIZE: usize = 200;\nconst STEPS: usize = 12_000;\n\n// Grid: false = white, true = black\nlet mut grid = vec![vec![false; SIZE]; SIZE];\n\n// Ant: position and direction (0=up, 1=right, 2=down, 3=left)\nlet (mut ax, mut ay, mut dir) = (SIZE / 2, SIZE / 2, 0u8);\n\nfor _ in 0..STEPS {\n    if grid[ay][ax] {\n        dir = (dir + 3) % 4; // On black: turn left\n    } else {\n        dir = (dir + 1) % 4; // On white: turn right\n    }\n    grid[ay][ax] = !grid[ay][ax]; // Flip cell color\n\n    // Move forward (toroidal wrapping)\n    match dir {\n        0 => ay = if ay == 0 { SIZE - 1 } else { ay - 1 },\n        1 => ax = if ax + 1 >= SIZE { 0 } else { ax + 1 },\n        2 => ay = if ay + 1 >= SIZE { 0 } else { ay + 1 },\n        3 => ax = if ax == 0 { SIZE - 1 } else { ax - 1 },\n        _ => unreachable!(),\n    }\n}\n\n// Render the grid as Canvas (scaled to 800×800)\nconst SCALE: u32 = 4;\nlet canvas = Canvas::from_fn(SIZE as u32 * SCALE, SIZE as u32 * SCALE, |x, y| {\n    let (ux, uy) = ((x / SCALE) as usize, (y / SCALE) as usize);\n    if ux == ax && uy == ay {\n        (220, 50, 50) // ant position: red\n    } else if grid[uy][ux] {\n        (30, 30, 40) // black cell\n    } else {\n        (200, 200, 200) // white cell\n    }\n});\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true,
      "saved_output": "[{\"Text\":\"(output too large to embed; run the cell to regenerate it)\"}]"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "The Highway Phenomenon",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The Highway Phenomenon\n\nAfter approximately 10,000 steps of seemingly chaotic wandering, the ant discovers a periodic diagonal highway and begins extending it indefinitely. This highway:\n\n- Has a **period of 104 steps**: after 104 moves, the pattern repeats, shifted diagonally.\n- Emerges **regardless of starting conditions** (as far as anyone has tested), though the onset time varies.\n- Remains **unproven**: no mathematical proof explains why the transition always occurs.\n\nThe messy region in the center is the initial chaotic phase. The diagonal stripe extending from it is the highway.\n\n### Try Modifying\n\n- Increase the step count to 50,000+ to see a longer highway.\n- Try starting on a non-blank grid (random initial cells), and the highway still emerges!\n- Implement **multi-color extensions** (e.g., \"LLRR\" or \"LRRRRRLLR\" rule strings) for dramatically different behavior.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Watch Langton's Ant traverse a grid, producing surprising order from simple turn rules.",
